{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<h1>Invites</h1>
<div class="doing">
  <a class="btn" href="{{newUrl}}">{{{plusIcon}}}Invite somebody</a>
</div>
<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{sent}}</div><div class="l">sent</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{opened}}</div><div class="l">opened</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{clicked}}</div><div class="l">clicked</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{converted}}</div><div class="l">joined</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{conversionRate}}%</div><div class="l">conversion</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{outstanding}}</div><div class="l">outstanding</div></div>
</div>
<details class="explain">
  <summary>What these numbers do and do not mean</summary>

  <p><strong>Sent</strong> and <strong>joined</strong> are facts. The server sent the message, and
  somebody signed up with that address. <strong>Conversion</strong> is the only number here worth
  making a decision on &mdash; not how many went out, but how many became a person who turns up.</p>

  <p><strong>Open is not a read, in either direction, and it has got worse.</strong> An open is
  recorded when a one-pixel image in the message is fetched. That fails both ways:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Under-reports.</b> Most clients block remote images by default. Somebody can read the
      whole thing, twice, and never register an open. <em>No evidence of an open</em> is exactly
      what it says &mdash; it is not "unread".</li>
    <li><b>Over-reports, which is newer.</b> Apple Mail Privacy Protection fetches remote images
      <em>on its own</em>, before a person has opened anything, and Apple Mail is around half of
      all opens. Gmail proxies and pre-fetches images too. So a recorded open frequently means a
      machine looked, not a person.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Read together: an open is <strong>weak evidence that the message was delivered somewhere real
  and was not obviously refused</strong>. It is not evidence that a human saw it. A high open rate
  with no conversions usually means Apple prefetching, not interest; a low open rate with good
  conversions is normal and fine.</p>

  <p><strong>Clicked is the one in between, and it is honest.</strong> It is recorded when somebody
  follows the link in the message and lands on the sign-up form &mdash; a decision a person made,
  which no mail client makes on their behalf. Read the three together and you know which problem you
  have: nothing opened is a message that did not arrive or did not get looked at; opened but never
  clicked is a message that did not persuade; clicked but never joined is a sign-up form or a moment
  that lost them, and that one is worth investigating because it is the closest anybody got.</p>

  <p>If you want to know whether an invitation worked, look at <strong>joined</strong>. It is the
  only number here that a machine cannot fake on somebody's behalf.</p>

  <p class="dim">The pixel is never cached and always returns an image, even for a token that means
  nothing &mdash; a mail client must not get an error page, and a cached pixel would record one open
  forever.</p>
</details>

<form class="tools" method="get" action="{{action}}" data-filters>
  <div class="search">{{{searchIcon}}}
    <input type="search" name="q" value="{{q}}" placeholder="Search invitations by email address" aria-label="search invitations">
  </div>
  <div class="chips" role="group" aria-label="stage">
    {{#stages}}<label class="chip"><input type="radio" name="stage" value="{{value}}"{{#selected}} checked{{/selected}}>{{label}}</label>{{/stages}}
  </div>
  <noscript><button type="submit" class="quiet">Filter</button></noscript>
</form>
<div class="panel" data-panel="{{panelUrl}}">{{{panel}}}</div>
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
